00:15:07,100 --> 00:15:10,040
But it's also a really hard dementia to live with.

235
00:15:11,200 --> 00:15:16,120
And yet it can look like it isn't if you're not careful.

236
00:15:16,300 --> 00:15:19,800
So we have several people in our community that live with FTD.

237
00:15:20,140 --> 00:15:27,400
And if you're curious and you want to know more about their perspective, how their story plays out, we have someone named Scott.

238
00:15:27,400 --> 00:15:28,840
We have someone named Ann.

239
00:15:28,960 --> 00:15:29,880
We have.

240
00:15:30,460 --> 00:15:31,360
We had.

241
00:15:31,520 --> 00:15:40,880
Someone named Lynn who lost her life due to communication issues with the medical community in her own health condition.

242
00:15:42,040 --> 00:15:46,700
But what we do know is learning about FTD is super important.

243
00:15:47,580 --> 00:15:49,700
Behavioral variant is a real challenge.

244
00:15:50,220 --> 00:15:56,240
So getting support for yourself and getting support for people you care about really important.

245
00:15:56,240 --> 00:16:01,140
And finding people who know how to do this and are willing to work with you.

246
00:16:01,360 --> 00:16:04,460
And are not seeing things in black and white, but shades of gray.

247
00:16:04,920 --> 00:16:09,280
And recognizing the art and the skill of supporting someone.

248
00:16:10,080 --> 00:16:10,760
Super tricky.

249
00:16:11,740 --> 00:16:14,100
And it's not easy.

250
00:16:14,640 --> 00:16:25,360
But there are things we can do when we can be in collaboration and moving forward that allow us to keep people a little safer, a little more secure, and a little less distressed.

251
